\textbf{Proof of Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}} (Routing Information Loss Under RevIN, revised form).

\begin{proof}

\label{app:learnable_alpha}

To test whether gradient descent independently discovers Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction, we parameterize the router input as $\mathbf{r} = \sigma(\ell)\,\text{RevIN}(\mathbf{X}) + (1 - \sigma(\ell))\,\mathbf{X}_{\text{raw}}$, where $\ell$ is a scalar \texttt{nn.Parameter} initialized to $0$ (so $\alpha = \sigma(0) = 0.5$). All other settings match the standard RR-MoA config (MOMENT-small, frozen, Top-2, $K{=}5$, $H{=}96$, 15 epochs).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Learned normalization coefficient} ($\alpha$, 5 seeds). All 30 runs converge to $\alpha < 0.5$, confirming gradient descent prefers raw input for routing.}
\label{tab:learnable_alpha}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Learned $\alpha$ (mean $\pm$ std) & MSE \\
\midrule
ETTh1       & $0.468 \pm 0.006$ & $0.463 \pm 0.015$ \\
ETTh2       & $0.482 \pm 0.007$ & $0.553 \pm 0.020$ \\
ETTm1       & $0.473 \pm 0.005$ & $0.397 \pm 0.012$ \\
ETTm2       & $0.466 \pm 0.003$ & $0.355 \pm 0.055$ \\
Electricity & $0.462 \pm 0.004$ & $0.269 \pm 0.008$ \\
Weather     & $0.459 \pm 0.009$ & $0.245 \pm 0.010$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All 30 runs converge to $\alpha < 0.5$ (range $0.445$--$0.491$), with trajectories monotonically decreasing from the $0.492$ initialization. The unanimity is the key finding: gradient descent independently discovers that raw input carries more routing information, confirming the qualitative prediction of Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}. The narrow converged range ($0.459$--$0.482$) means the magnitude does not strongly differentiate datasets---the signal is binary (raw $>$ norm), not graded.

