\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] \textbf{Exact decomposition.} $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S) \geq 0$.
    \item[\textup{(ii)}] \textbf{Unconditional quantitative bound.} Without any independence assumption,
    \[
        I(X; E) - I(S; E) \;\geq\; I(M, \Sigma;\, E) \;-\; I(M, \Sigma;\, S),
    \]
    where the residual $\varepsilon := I(M, \Sigma;\, S) \geq 0$ measures the ``leakage'' of stripped statistics through the shape.  Equality (tightness) holds iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ given $E$.
    \item[\textup{(iii)}] \textbf{Full-information corner.} If the router depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics ($E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$), then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the \emph{entire} routing distribution in nats.
    \item[\textup{(iv)}] \textbf{Empirical leakage and signal ratio.} The leakage $\varepsilon$ varies by dataset ($0.09$--$1.26$ nats via CCA; cross-validated within ${\pm}0.2$ nats by the distribution-free KSG estimator~\citep{kraskov2004ksg}). The signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ serves as a monotone proxy for the \emph{potential} routing information in the stripped statistics---validated at $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ across nine datasets.
    \item[\textup{(v)}] \textbf{Direct measurement and tightness.} Since $E$ is deterministic given $X$, $I(X;E){=}H(E)$ and the exact MI loss (Part~i) reduces to $H(E \mid S)$, measurable via held-out cross-entropy of a classifier $S \to E$ (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness}). Across nine datasets, normalization retains only $16$--$41\%$ of routing entropy. The bound~(ii) gap $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ averages $0.075$ nats ($4.7\%$ of $\log K$), confirming tightness.\footnote{$H(E \mid S)$ does \emph{not} correlate with $\Delta\%$ ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$, $p{=}0.77$); $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts improvement because it measures routing \emph{potential}, not the MI loss itself.}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. \textit{Remark.} $R$ predicts improvement ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$) because it measures how much routing-useful information the statistics \emph{could carry}, not how much the router \emph{actually loses}---the distinction between Part~(iv) and~(v). KSG cross-check (${\pm}0.26$ nats) confirms the leakage estimates hold beyond Gaussianity. \textbf{Independent validation:} training $\alpha \in [0,1]$ (raw vs.\ RevIN interpolation) as an \texttt{nn.Parameter}, gradient descent converges to $\alpha < 0.5$ on all six datasets ($30/30$ runs; Appendix~\ref{app:learnable_alpha}).

